and a pair, which is All Chows, worth 2 points. Also, since all are in different suits (T, B, and W?), but the W2 and W3 are in the hand. Wait, I have W2 and W3. If I keep them, maybe they can form a chow with another tile. But if I play T6, I have to see if I can form a hand with four chows. Let's see:\n\nAfter playing T6, the hand would be W2, W3, T2, T3, T4, T5, B5, B6, B7, F3, F4, J3, J3. That's 13 tiles. Then I need to draw one. Suppose I draw a tile that completes a chow. For example, if I draw a W1, I can form W1-W2-W3. Then the hand would have three chows in T (T2-3-4 and T3-4-5), one in B (B5-6-7), and one in W (W1-2-3), plus the J3 pair. That's four chows and a pair, which is All Chows (2 points). But All Chows requires all four melds to be chows. However, the W2 and W3 are only two tiles; unless I get a third, but if I draw W1, then that's a chow. So playing T6 might set up for that.\n\nAlternatively, if I play B7, then I have B5-B6 left. But then maybe I can't form the B5-6-7 chow. So keeping B5-B6-B7 is better for forming that chow. \n\nAnother option is to play W2 or W3. If I play W2, then I can't form a chow with W2-W3. But if I keep both, maybe I can form a chow if I get a W1 or W4. But since the unshown tiles have W1:4, W4:4, there are plenty of W1 and W4 left. So maybe it's better to keep the W2 and W3 and try to form a chow later.\n\nLooking at the valid actions, the options include playing T6, W3, W2, T4, etc. If I play T6, I can focus on the T and B chows. If I play W3, then I lose the chance to form a chow with W2 and W3. Similarly for W2. \n\nAnother consideration is the possibility of forming a Seven Pairs. The user has two J3s, so if other pairs can be formed. For example, T2, T3, T4, T5, T6 could form pairs if they have two each, but currently they are single. So Seven Pairs is not feasible unless they discard other tiles to form pairs. But with the current hand, it's not likely. So probably focusing on All Chows is better.\n\nAlso, the hand has a pair of J3s. If the hand is All Chows, which is 2 points, plus the pair is part of the All Chows. But All Chows requires the pair to be suited. Wait, the pair in All Chows must be suited tiles. The J3 is a honor tile. So that would not qualify for All Chows. Wait, no. All Chows requires four chows and a pair of suited tiles. But the pair here is J3, a dragon, which is a honor tile. Therefore, the hand as is would not qualify for All Chows. So that's a problem. Hmm.\n\nWait, maybe I need to adjust. If I have four chows and a pair of suited tiles. The current pair is J3, which is a honor tile. So if I can form a pair of suited tiles instead. For example, if I have two J3s, but that's a honor tile. So maybe I need to discard the J3s and form another pair. But the user has two J3s, which is a pair. So if I want All Chows, the pair must be suited. Therefore, I need to discard one of the J3s to form a suited pair. But that's not in the current valid actions. The valid actions are to play T6, W3, etc. So maybe playing F3 or F4 to discard a wind tile, but those are in the valid actions. Wait, the valid actions include Play F3. If I play F3, then the pair is F4 and J3? No, because F4 is one, and J3 is two. Wait, the hand after playing F3 would have F4 and J3 as two pairs? But that's two pairs. But the hand needs four chows and one pair. Hmm. Maybe not helpful.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the user can form a hand with three chows and a pair of J3, but that's not All Chows. So perhaps the All Chows is not possible here. Then what's the next best option?\n\nLooking at other patterns. The hand has a sequence in T2-T6 and B5-B7. If I can form a Full Flush (all in one suit), but the hand has multiple suits. So that's not possible. Maybe All Chows is not feasible because of the pair. Then perhaps focusing on other patterns. \n\nAnother possibility is Mixed Straight, which requires chows in different suits for 1-2-3, 4-5-6, 7-8-9. The user has T2-T3-T4 (2-3-4), T4-T5-T6 (4-5-6),