nd F4. If they can form a pung of F3 or F4, but they only have one each. So not helpful. \n\nWhat about the Seat Wind? The user's seat wind is East. They have F3 (West?) No, F1 is East, F2 is South, F3 is West, F4 is North. The user has F3 (West Wind) and F4 (North Wind). If they can form a pung of their seat wind (East), but they don't have any. So Seat Wind is not applicable. \n\nPrevalent Wind is West. If they can form a pung of West Wind (F3), which they have one. Not enough. \n\nSo perhaps the best bet is to play T6 to keep the potential for multiple chows. Even if the pair is a honor tile, maybe other patterns can be formed. For example, if the user can form a hand with three chows and a pair, and some other points. \n\nAlternatively, playing W3 might help if they can get a W4 to form a chow. But with W3 in hand, they need W2 and W4, but they already have W2. So if they keep W3, they can form a chow with W2 and W3 if they get W1 or W4. \n\nBut the current hand has T2-T6 and B5-B7. Playing T6 might be the best to keep the T chows. So the answer is Play T6.\n", "output": "Evaluation: Playing T6 allows forming multiple chows in T and B suits, potentially leading to an All Chows hand. It maintains flexibility for concealed melds and avoids disrupting sequential tiles.\nReason: Discarding T6 preserves the T2-T5 sequence and B5-B7 sequence, which can form chows. Keeping W2 and W3 allows potential for a W chow if a W1 or W4 is drawn. The J3 pair is a honor tile, but the focus on chows maximizes scoring potential. \nAnswer: Play T6"}
