lity of that chow. \n\nAlternatively, if I discard T5, which has 3 left. T5 is part of T4-T5. If I discard T5, I still have T2-T4. \n\nHmm. Maybe the best is to discard a tile that's part of a longer sequence but has more copies left. Let's see:\n\n- T4: 3 left. B6: 4 left. B7: 4 left. \n\nIf I discard B6, there are 4 left, so it's safe. If I discard B6, I still have B5, B7, B8. So B5-B7-B8 could form a chow if I get a B6. \n\nWait, but I have B5, B6, B7, B8. If I discard B6, I can still have B5, B7, B8. But to form a chow, I need three consecutive. So B5-B6-B7 or B6-B7-B8. If I discard B6, I can't form either. So that's bad. \n\nSo maybe it's better to keep B6. \n\nSimilarly, if I discard B7, I have B5, B6, B8. No chow. So that's worse. \n\nSo maybe discarding B8 is better. If I discard B8, I have B5, B6, B7. Which can form a chow. \n\nAh, right! If I discard B8, I still have B5, B6, B7. That's a chow. So discarding B8 would leave me with a potential chow in B5-B6-B7. \n\nSo that's a good move. Because keeping the B5-B6-B7 chow is valuable. \n\nSo the valid action is Play B8. \n\nLet me check the unshown tiles: B8 has 3 left. So discarding B8 is safe. \n\nYes, that seems like a good choice. It preserves the B5-B6-B7 chow and removes a tile that's part of a longer sequence but not needed for that chow. \n\nSo the answer is Play B8.\n", "output": "Evaluation: Discard B8 to preserve the B5-B6-B7 chow potential and reduce risk of others completing their hands.\nReason: B8 is part of a longer sequence but discarding it allows keeping B5-B6-B7 as a complete chow. B8 has 3 remaining copies, making it relatively safe to discard. This maintains flexibility in the Dots suit while avoiding exposing critical tiles for others' melds.\nAnswer: Play B8"}
