scarding B7 could allow someone to Chi if they have B6 and B8. B6 has 1 left, B8 has 1. If someone has B6 and B8, they could Chi. But I have B5, so if I discard B7, maybe they have B6 and B8. But since B6 and B8 are in low supply, maybe it's risky. Alternatively, keeping B7 as a terminal tile.\n\nPlay B5: I have B5. The unshown B5 has 2 left. If I discard B5, someone might Chi if they have B4 and B6 or B6 and B7. B4 is 1 left, B6 is 1. If they have B4 and B6, they could form a Chow with B5. But I have B5 and B7. So maybe it's safer to keep B5.\n\nNow, considering my current hand. I need to form two more melds and a pair. Let's see:\n\nCurrent hand: W2, W4, T2, T3, T7, B1, B5, B7.\n\nPossible melds:\n\n- W2 and W4: If I draw W3, I can form a Chow of W2, W3, W4. But I already have a Pung of W1, W2, W3. Wait, no, the Pung is W1, W2, W3. So W4 is separate. So maybe W4 could be part of a different meld. But I don't have W3 or W5 with W4 to form a Chow. So maybe W4 is a single tile.\n\n- T2 and T3: If I draw T1, I can form a Chow of T1, T2, T3. But T1 has 1 left. If I discard T2 or T3, someone might Chi if they have the other tile. For example, if I discard T2, someone with T1 and T3 could Chi. But I have T3 in my hand. If I discard T3, someone with T2 and T4 could Chi, but T4 is already out. So maybe keeping T2 and T3 for a possible Chow if I draw T1.\n\n- B1: If I have B1, B2, B3, but I don't have those. So not helpful.\n\n- B5 and B7: If I have B5 and B7, maybe if I draw B6, I can form a Chow. B6 has 1 left. So if I keep B5 and B7, and draw B6, that's a Chow. But I have B5 in my hand. So maybe keeping B5 is useful for that.\n\nSo, considering the possible melds, maybe keeping B5 is better for forming a Chow with B4, B5, B6 or B5, B6, B7 if I draw B6. But B6 has only 1 left. If I discard B5, someone might take it for a Chow if they have B4 or B6.\n\nAlternatively, if I discard a tile that's less useful. For example, T7 is a terminal. If I discard T7, I can't form a pair