ealed kong of J3 (8 points) and a chow of W1-W2-W3 (2 points). Discarding W2, part of the completed chow, allows retaining flexibility for higher-value melds or pairs while minimizing risk of others completing their hands.  \nReason: Playing W2 removes a redundant tile from a completed chow, preserving the pair of B7 and open possibilities for additional pungs or chows. The kong of J3 and existing chow already contribute 10 points, meeting the minimum requirement, but further optimization is possible.  \nAnswer: Play W2"}
